{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -i 'fallopian tube.*progenitor|oviduct.*progenitor|uterine tube.*progenitor|epcam|pecam1|cd31|cd326' src/ontology/cl-edit.owl\"","aggregated_output":"AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000134 \"Many but not all mesenchymal cells derive from the mesoderm. MSCs are reportedly CD3-negative, CD4-negative, CD5-negative, CD8-negative, CD11a-negative, CD11b-negative, CD14-negative, CD19-negative, CD29-positive, CD31-negative, CD34-negative, CD38-negative, CD40-negative, CD44-positive, CD45-negative, CD49-positive, CD54-positive, CD66b-negative, CD79a-negative, CD80-negative, CD102-positive, CD106-positive, CD117-positive, CD121a-positive, CD121b-positive, CD123-positive, CD124-positive, CD133-negative, CD146-positive, CD166-positive, CD271-positive, B220-negative, Gr1-negative, MHCI-positive, MHCII-negative, SSEA4-negative, sca1-positive, Ter119-negative, and glycophorin A-negative. Cultured MSCs are capable of producing stem cell factor, IL7, IL8, IL11, TGF-beta, cofilin, galectin-1, laminin-receptor 1, cyclophilin A, and MMP-2.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000566 \"These cells are reportedly CD31-positive, CD34-positive, CD144-positive, CD309-positive, and TAL1-positive.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000669 \"Pericytes are CD10-positive, CD13-positive, CD31-negative, CD45-negative, CD106-positive, CD117-negative, CD140-positive, CD144-negative, CD146-positive, CD271-positive, CD325-positive, NG2-positive, RGS5-positive, SMA-positive, and desmin-positive. A subpopulation is CD248-positive. They are also capable of producing angiopoietin 1, CXCL12, TGF-beta, and VEGF-A.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000889 \"Markers: Mouse: CD11b+GR1+CD31+; Human: CD34+ CD33+CD15-CD13+. (According to some reports in humans these cells are iNOS+ARG1+IL13+IFNg+); location: In cancerous tissue; in the blood and lymphoid organs in sepsis.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000980 \"This cell type is compatible with the HIPC Lyoplate markers for 'plasmablast'. Plasmablasts are also reportedly CD48-positive, CD63-positive, CD229-positive, CD270-positive, CD319-positive, CD352-positive, CD361-positive, and IgD-negative.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0002139 \"These cells are reportedly CD31-positive, CD34-positive, CD144-positive, TAL1-positive.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:11250738\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19022771\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:38059556\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002324 \"A myoepithelial cell that is part of a mammary gland and is located in the basal layer. During lactation, a basal-myoepithelial cell of mammary gland contracts under the stimulation of oxytocin. In humans, a basal-myoepithelial cell of mammary gland can be identified by high levels of CD49f and low levels of EpCAM.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:21435450\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:22566825\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25998961\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:28262465\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:29752062\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34256989\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7655-4833\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0009102 \"A specialized, fibroblastic reticular cell of mesenchymal origin found in lymph nodes. In human, it expresses several markers common to myofibroblasts (desmin, vimentin, CD73, CD90, α-smooth muscle actin (αSMA)), and can be differentiated from endothelial cells by its lack of CD31 expression. These cells are critical for the overall organization and function of the lymph node. Lymph node fibroblastic reticular cells (FRCs) can be further classified based on their location, function, and unique marker expression.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25311230\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4030033 \"At least in some mammalian species, a valve endothelial cell expresses CD31. Valve endothelial cells can undergo an endothelial to mesenchymal transition to acquire a fibroblast or myofibroblast phenotype that leads to changes in the microenvironmental signals and facilitates tissue regeneration.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:2210508\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:22911892\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30023181\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033014 \"A small, narrow, peg-shaped epithelial cell with little cytoplasm that is part of oviduct epithelium. This cell is rarer than the ciliated and secretory epithelial cells of the fallopian tube epithelium and is often found intercalated between them. Peg cells are generally distributed basally along the epithelium and have been found in high concentrations at the fimbriated, distal end of the fallopian tube in humans. It may have a regenerative/stem-cell function. In humans, markers include EPCAM, CD44, and ITGA6.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19648928\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35617956\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://nrs.harvard.edu/URN-3:HUL.INSTREPOS:37368328\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6677-8489\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033057 \"A luminal epithelial cell of the mammary gland that can proliferate and has the potential to differentiate into a lactocyte during pregnancy. In humans, a luminal adaptive secretory precursor cell can be identified by high levels of the markers EpCAM and CD49f, and in mice it can be identified by low levels of CD29 and high levels of CD14, Kit, CD61, and Tspan8.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19648928\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:26390871\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34156490\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://nrs.harvard.edu/URN-3:HUL.INSTREPOS:37368328\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6677-8489\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033058 \"A luminal epithelial cell of the mammary gland that transduces endocrine cues to orchestrate proliferation, architectural remodeling, and differentiation of other cells in the mammary gland via paracrine signaling. This cell expresses high levels of estrogen receptors. In humans, a luminal hormone-sensing cell can be identified by high levels of EpCAM and low levels of CD49f, and in mice it can be identified by low levels of CD29 and high levels of Foxa1, CD133, and Sca1 (Ly6a).\")\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
